1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,600
Your tap still runs when the power dies.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:05,640
But why does it stop after about
twenty-four hours...

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,350
and what do you lose first?

4
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:08,980
This is a what-if.

5
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,080
Most blackouts end in hours.

6
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,320
This one doesn't.

7
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,570
We're going to follow it for seven days...

8
00:00:14,610 --> 00:00:15,750
hour by hour...

9
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:19,400
using the rules from the CDC, FEMA and the
USDA.

10
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:20,520
And stay to the end.

11
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,610
Because the most dangerous moment isn't
when the lights go out.

12
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:25,140
It's when they come back on...

13
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:26,750
and you open the fridge.

14
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:29,610
Hour one. Your lights go out.

15
00:00:29,650 --> 00:00:31,580
That's the part everyone expects.

16
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:32,740
What comes next...

17
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:34,360
is what nobody does.

18
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:36,120
Your card stops working.

19
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,110
Card readers and ATMs need power and a
network.

20
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:40,880
So it's cash only.

21
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:42,490
Then the gas station.

22
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:44,480
Here's the part that surprises people.

23
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:46,290
The station isn't out of gas.

24
00:00:46,330 --> 00:00:48,180
The tanks under the ground are full.

25
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:52,150
But the only thing that lifts that fuel to
your car is an electric pump.

26
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:54,170
No power... no pump.

27
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:55,780
A few states plan for this.

28
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:59,917
Florida, for example, requires many
stations near highways and evacuation

29
00:00:59,957 --> 00:01:01,850
routes to be wired for a generator.

30
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:04,310
Most of the country has no rule like that.

31
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:05,370
And your fridge?

32
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:06,730
It starts a countdown.

33
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:10,850
Hour six. Your fridge just passed its
four-hour mark.

34
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:15,630
The USDA rule: food in a closed fridge
stays safe for about four hours.

35
00:01:15,670 --> 00:01:19,550
After that, meat, milk, eggs and leftovers
have to go.

36
00:01:19,590 --> 00:01:21,560
Your freezer is your best friend now.

37
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,950
A full one holds for about forty-eight
hours.

38
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:26,200
Half full? Just twenty-four.

39
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:28,200
Every traffic light is dark.

40
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:32,400
Many states write it into law: a dead
signal means an all-way stop.

41
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:34,250
And your phone still has signal...

42
00:01:34,290 --> 00:01:36,610
for now. Here's why that won't last.

43
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:38,480
A cell tower looks like a pole.

44
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:39,840
It's really a small building...

45
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:42,310
with a room full of batteries at the
bottom.

46
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:46,630
Industry guidelines have called for as
little as four hours of backup, with eight

47
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:47,220
as the goal.

48
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:51,960
After Hurricane Katrina, the FCC tried to
require eight hours nationwide.

49
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,570
That rule never took effect.

50
00:01:53,610 --> 00:01:55,540
So the towers are draining.

51
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:57,750
And so is something you can't see.

52
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:01,300
Hour twelve. Your phone is at forty
percent...

53
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:02,950
and it's dropping fast.

54
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:05,760
Your Wi-Fi router died the moment the
power did.

55
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:07,920
So your phone leans on the cell network.

56
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:09,810
But the towers are weaker now.

57
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:13,030
As the signal drops, your phone works
harder to stay connected...

58
00:02:13,070 --> 00:02:14,900
and that burns battery.

59
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:16,960
And everyone is calling at once.

60
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,550
The network jams.

61
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:22,420
The FCC's advice: keep calls short, and
text instead.

62
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,870
A text can get through when a call can't.

63
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:26,670
You can charge your phone in your car.

64
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:29,360
But only with the car outside, in the
open.

65
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:30,900
Never in the garage.

66
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:31,810
Hold that thought...

67
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:33,330
it matters on day two.

68
00:02:33,370 --> 00:02:34,890
But by hour twenty-four...

69
00:02:34,930 --> 00:02:36,500
the problem isn't your phone.

70
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:37,730
It's your water.

71
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:39,790
Hour twenty-four.

72
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:42,630
So why did your tap keep running all this
time?

73
00:02:42,670 --> 00:02:44,540
Follow the pipe under your street.

74
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:45,710
It climbs a hill...

75
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:47,310
to a tank on legs.

76
00:02:47,350 --> 00:02:48,450
A water tower.

77
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:50,140
The water up there is heavy.

78
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:53,160
Its own weight pushes it through every
pipe in town.

79
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,640
That's your water pressure.

80
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:56,650
No motor. Just gravity.

81
00:02:56,690 --> 00:02:58,090
Unless you live up high.

82
00:02:58,130 --> 00:03:02,090
Many tall buildings use electric pumps to
push water to the upper floors.

83
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:04,760
Up there, the tap can quit in the first
hour.

84
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,380
But something had to lift that water up
there.

85
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:08,690
Electric pumps.

86
00:03:08,730 --> 00:03:10,490
And they stopped when the lights did.

87
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:14,350
Many systems are built to hold about one
day of normal use up high.

88
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:16,120
So turn on your tap...

89
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:19,830
and listen. As the tower drains, the
pressure drops.

90
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:22,180
And pressure is what keeps the pipes
clean.

91
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:25,950
When it falls, dirty water can seep in
through tiny cracks.

92
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:28,260
That's when a boil-water notice goes out.

93
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:32,190
The CDC's rule: a rolling boil for one
full minute.

94
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:34,530
This is why the first thing to do in a
blackout...

95
00:03:34,570 --> 00:03:36,150
is fill everything.

96
00:03:36,190 --> 00:03:37,490
Pots. Bottles.

97
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:41,940
The bathtub. Ready.gov says one gallon per
person, per day.

98
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,410
And at hour forty-eight...

99
00:03:43,450 --> 00:03:45,560
the real danger isn't hunger.

100
00:03:45,910 --> 00:03:47,420
Hour forty-eight.

101
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:49,900
Even a full freezer is giving up now.

102
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:52,510
So people start cooking however they can.

103
00:03:52,550 --> 00:03:54,330
Grills. Camp stoves.

104
00:03:54,370 --> 00:03:58,350
Generators. And this is where blackouts
turn deadly.

105
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:00,460
Carbon monoxide has no smell...

106
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,390
and no color. It doesn't make you cough.

107
00:04:03,430 --> 00:04:05,230
The first signs are a headache...

108
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:07,400
dizziness... feeling tired.

109
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:10,400
In the dark, on day two, that feels
normal.

110
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:12,020
So people lie down.

111
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:15,150
And the garage isn't the safe spot people
think it is.

112
00:04:15,190 --> 00:04:18,530
The CDC says don't even run a car in an
attached garage...

113
00:04:18,570 --> 00:04:20,100
with the door open.

114
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:24,304
The CDC counts more than four hundred
deaths a year in the US from carbon

115
00:04:24,344 --> 00:04:26,730
monoxide that has nothing to do with
fires.

116
00:04:26,770 --> 00:04:32,093
The rule: never run a generator, a grill
or a camp stove inside your home, your

117
00:04:32,133 --> 00:04:33,740
garage, or near a window.

118
00:04:33,780 --> 00:04:37,060
Keep a generator at least twenty feet from
doors and windows.

119
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:40,920
And a battery-powered CO alarm is the only
thing that will warn you.

120
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:42,580
And at hour seventy-two...

121
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:44,110
the cold moves in.

122
00:04:44,460 --> 00:04:46,090
Hour seventy-two.

123
00:04:46,130 --> 00:04:50,670
Day three. If it's winter, your house gets
colder every hour.

124
00:04:50,710 --> 00:04:53,570
Never use your oven or gas stove to heat
your home.

125
00:04:53,610 --> 00:04:55,680
Ready.gov warns against it.

126
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:57,600
Instead: pick one room.

127
00:04:57,640 --> 00:04:58,840
Close off the rest.

128
00:04:58,880 --> 00:04:59,990
Cover the windows.

129
00:05:00,030 --> 00:05:02,410
Layer up, and keep everyone together.

130
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:06,960
A small closed room holds the heat of a
few people far longer than a whole house.

131
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,330
And skip the candles.

132
00:05:08,370 --> 00:05:11,370
Ready.gov's advice is flashlights and
lanterns.

133
00:05:11,410 --> 00:05:16,424
A candle next to blankets, in a dark house
full of tired people, is a fire waiting to

134
00:05:16,464 --> 00:05:16,790
happen.

135
00:05:16,830 --> 00:05:22,933
Watch for the warning signs the CDC lists:
shivering, exhaustion, confusion, fumbling

136
00:05:22,973 --> 00:05:25,340
hands, slurred speech, drowsiness.

137
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:27,580
If you see those, you don't wait it out.

138
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:31,220
You go to a warming center, or anywhere
that still has power.

139
00:05:31,570 --> 00:05:35,100
Day five. The gas in your tank is running
low...

140
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:37,120
and the stations still can't pump.

141
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:39,840
Most stores depend on constant deliveries.

142
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:42,730
Without fuel and power, the shelves stay
empty.

143
00:05:42,770 --> 00:05:45,420
And there's a problem almost nobody thinks
about.

144
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:50,020
Sewage. Sewer systems rely on electric
pumps called lift stations.

145
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:53,580
FEMA warns that in a long outage, sewage
levels rise...

146
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:54,680
and can back up.

147
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:57,430
Cash is the only payment that still works.

148
00:05:57,470 --> 00:05:58,470
If you have any.

149
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:01,250
Day seven. One week.

150
00:06:01,290 --> 00:06:03,280
Hospitals switch to generators.

151
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:06,260
Many plan for about ninety-six hours on
their own.

152
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:09,440
After that, everything depends on fuel
deliveries.

153
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:12,110
So how do you restart a dead grid?

154
00:06:12,150 --> 00:06:13,480
Here's the strange part.

155
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:16,590
Most big power plants need electricity to
start.

156
00:06:16,630 --> 00:06:18,510
A few special plants don't.

157
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:20,180
It's called a black start.

158
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:21,840
They energize one line...

159
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:23,110
wake up the next plant...

160
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:25,790
and rebuild the grid, piece by piece.

161
00:06:25,830 --> 00:06:26,900
It takes time.

162
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:31,580
In two thousand three, a blackout hit
about fifty million people in the US and

163
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:31,950
Canada.

164
00:06:31,990 --> 00:06:34,690
Some areas waited four days for power.

165
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:37,650
And then... the lights come back on.

166
00:06:37,690 --> 00:06:39,320
Your fridge is humming again.

167
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:40,970
The food feels cold.

168
00:06:41,010 --> 00:06:42,380
So people eat it.

169
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:43,830
That's the mistake.

170
00:06:43,870 --> 00:06:46,500
Cold now doesn't mean it stayed cold.

171
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:51,997
If your fridge was off for more than four
hours, meat, milk, eggs and leftovers have

172
00:06:52,037 --> 00:06:52,340
to go.

173
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:53,780
Your freezer is different.

174
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:56,890
If the food still has ice crystals, you
can refreeze it.

175
00:06:56,930 --> 00:06:58,870
Here's how to know for sure next time.

176
00:06:58,910 --> 00:07:01,480
Keep a cheap appliance thermometer in your
freezer.

177
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:06,272
If it still reads forty degrees or below,
the USDA says that food is safe to

178
00:07:06,312 --> 00:07:06,790
refreeze.

179
00:07:06,830 --> 00:07:09,360
And never taste food to check if it's
safe.

180
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:13,150
The USDA's rule is simple: when in doubt,
throw it out.

181
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:17,260
So if your lights go out tonight, here's
the first ten minutes.

182
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:19,380
Fill the tub and every pot.

183
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:21,550
Keep the fridge and freezer shut.

184
00:07:21,590 --> 00:07:23,990
Get cash while the ATM still works.

185
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:26,320
Charge your phone, and switch to texts.

186
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:29,100
And make sure your CO alarm runs on
batteries.

187
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,350
Seven days. Eight systems.

188
00:07:31,390 --> 00:07:33,300
All of it runs on one thing...

189
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:34,430
until it doesn't.
